system_prompt:
You are a basketball analytics researcher deciding whether and how a research question should be studied.
You work only from the task statement and the tools listed below. Do not assume facts you have not seen.

Required procedure, in this order:
1. Identify the research question.
2. Perform the prescribed retrieval step: call retrieve_history with the research question and a search query. This
   step is mandatory and comes before any other tool. In some settings it returns no records; then say so.
3. Summarise the relevant prior findings, failed experiments, methodological limitations and unresolved questions
   that the retrieval returned. Cite only identifiers that appeared in tool results. Leave lists empty if nothing
   relevant was returned; never invent prior work.
4. Explain how that information (or its absence) affects your assessment.
5. Make your final research decision.

Tools:
- retrieve_history(research_question, query, k=8): the prescribed retrieval step. Returns the most relevant records of earlier research by this project that are available in this setting, with their identifiers; returns an empty list when none are available.
- glossary(): definitions of basketball and project terms used in task statements.
- data_catalog(): the fields available in each per-game data file.
- read_document(key): full text of one section (by section id such as 'FAM:SF12') or one document (such as 'families/SF12').

Protocol: every reply is exactly one JSON object and nothing else.
To call a tool: {"action": "tool", "tool": "<name>", "args": {...}}
To finish:      {"action": "final", "output": <object following the schema below>}
You may call at most 12 tools; then you must finish. Check that every opening brace has a closing brace.

Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_question": "the research question in one sentence, in your own words",
 "retrieval_summary": {
  "prior_findings": [
   {
    "id": "identifier of the retrieved record",
    "status": "supported | rejected | untestable | artifact | uncertain",
    "summary": "what it found"
   }
  ],
  "failed_experiments": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "status": "rejected | untestable | artifact | uncertain",
    "summary": "why it failed"
   }
  ],
  "limitations": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "text": "methodological or data limitation"
   }
  ],
  "unresolved": [
   {
    "id": "identifier",
    "text": "question left open"
   }
  ]
 },
 "evidence_use": "how the retrieved information (or its absence) changed your assessment",
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}

Risk codes (use only these): SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER, EXPOSURE_SPARSITY, MEASURE_RELIABILITY, RTM_SELECTION, OUTCOME_DERIVED_LABEL, LEAKAGE_LOOKAHEAD, MECHANICAL_FLOOR, BASE_RATE, CONFOUNDING, LABEL_ERROR, PLACEBO_DEPENDENCE, MULTIPLE_TESTING, IMPLEMENTATION_BUG, DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY
Field guidance:
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

messages.content:
{
 "action": "tool",
 "tool": "retrieve_history",
 "args": {
  "research_question": "Do the roles of cut-heavy shapers transfer more effectively to new contexts (e.g., different teams, seasons) than the roles of drive-heavy shapers?",
  "query": "shapers role transferability cut-heavy drive-heavy"
 }
}

{
 "research_question": "Do the roles of cut-heavy shapers transfer more effectively to new contexts (e.g., different teams, seasons) than the roles of drive-heavy shapers?",
 "retrieval_summary": {
  "prior_findings": [
   {
    "id": "RES:APPA-052",
    "status": "supported",
    "summary": "Games with high CUT activity (CUT > 15%) are very rare (only 4 out of 272 games), suggesting that 'cut-heavy' as a game-level characteristic is uncommon."
   }
  ],
  "failed_experiments": [
   {
    "id": "RES:APPM-025",
    "status": "artifact",
    "summary": "An optimizer's attempt to generate CUT-heavy openers resulted in an artifact where all teams received the same CUT-heavy target, significantly exceeding their actual capacity, because it ignored personnel. This indicates a risk in defining 'cut-heavy' based on theoretical targets rather than actual player output or contextual capacity."
   }
  ],
  "limitations": [
   {
    "id": "RES:APPA-052",
    "text": "The rarity of games with high CUT activity (CUT > 15%, only 4 out of 272 games) implies a potential sample size issue for studying 'cut-heavy' contexts or players."
   }
  ],
  "unresolved": []
 },
 "evidence_use": "The retrieved information highlights a significant challenge: the rarity of 'cut-heavy' contexts as noted in RES:APPA-052. If 'cut-heavy' games are uncommon, it is likely that consistently 'cut-heavy shapers' are also rare, severely limiting the sample size for studying their role transferability. RES:APPM-025 also provides a crucial warning against defining 'cut-heavy' based on theoretical targets without considering actual player capabilities and team context, reinforcing the need for an empirically grounded definition based on observed play. The absence of directly relevant prior work on shaper role transferability means this is a novel question, but the data sparsity for 'cut-heavy' players is a major concern.",
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": 0.1,
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
    "text": "The rarity of 'cut-heavy' game contexts (RES:APPA-052) strongly suggests that 'cut-heavy shapers' will be very scarce. This will lead to insufficient data points to reliably identify such players and observe their role transfer across different team/season contexts with useful statistical power."
   },
   {
    "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
    "text": "The 'n=24' units available, combined with the expected sparsity of 'cut-heavy' players, makes it highly improbable to achieve sufficient statistical power to detect meaningful differences in role transferability between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers."
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "no",
  "cited": [],
  "summary": "No prior work directly addresses the differential transferability of cut-heavy versus drive-heavy shaper roles. However, existing research (RES:APPA-052, RES:APPM-025) indicates that 'cut-heavy' situations are rare and that defining 'cut-heavy' without considering personnel capacity can lead to artifacts, which are relevant methodological considerations."
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
   "text": "Identifying a sufficient number of 'cut-heavy shapers' who change teams or significantly alter team context will be challenging due to the observed rarity of 'cut-heavy' play patterns."
  },
  {
   "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
   "text": "Even if some 'cut-heavy shapers' are identified, the sample size for observing role transfer (i.e., players moving between teams or having significant scheme changes) will likely be too small to draw statistically significant conclusions."
  },
  {
   "code": "CONFOUNDING",
   "text": "Player roles and transferability are highly confounded by surrounding talent, coaching schemes, and individual player development/decline. Controlling for these factors effectively with limited data will be difficult."
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": 0.05,
  "rejected": 0.05,
  "untestable": 0.8,
  "artifact": 0.05,
  "uncertain": 0.05
 },
 "decision": "do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "The difference in the change in (per-possession efficiency, play-type usage, or role contribution) for shapers classified as 'cut-heavy' vs. 'drive-heavy' when they transition to a new team or experience a significant change in team context (e.g., coaching change, major roster overhaul).",
  "unit": "Player-season transitions (player-team-season A to player-team-season B).",
  "sample": "All players identified as 'shapers' (e.g., top 20% in usage rate or offensive touches) across the NBA 2022-23 to 2025-26 seasons who change teams or experience a defined significant team context change. Players would be classified as 'cut-heavy' or 'drive-heavy' based on the proportion of their initiated possessions ending in cuts vs. drives, using play-by-play and derived play-type data. A minimum threshold (e.g., >10% of initiated possessions for the dominant type) would be required for classification.",
  "power": "Not feasible to achieve useful power given the expected sparsity of 'cut-heavy shapers' and relevant transitions. A pilot study would be needed to even estimate the population size of such players.",
  "controls": "Player-level controls: prior season efficiency, age, draft position, position. Team-level controls: team offensive rating, pace, presence of other star players. Context controls: change in coach, change in teammates' roles. Regression adjustment or matching on observable covariates if sample size permits.",
  "falsification": "Examine if the observed 'transferability' is merely a function of player quality (e.g., better players transfer better regardless of style) rather than the specific 'cut-heavy' vs 'drive-heavy' distinction. Test sensitivity to definitions of 'shaper' and 'heavy' play-type usage.",
  "multiplicity": "Given the likely small sample, avoid multiple comparisons. Focus on a single primary outcome metric (e.g., change in Net Offensive Rating contributed by the player, adjusted for team context) and a single primary hypothesis test.",
  "decision_rule": "Reject the null hypothesis (no difference in transferability) if the 95% confidence interval for the difference between cut-heavy and drive-heavy shapers' role transfer metrics does not contain zero, and the effect size is considered practically meaningful. However, given the identified risks, statistical significance is unlikely to be achievable.",
  "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2023-24 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2024-25 regular season and playoffs, NBA 2025-26 regular season (complete from 2026-04-13), NBA 2025-26 playoffs (complete from 2026-06-21). Per-game files from NBA stats API and derived play-type possession records."
 }
}

